                <pb facs="0715" n="627" sameAs="frus:frus1892/pg627"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 337.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Clipping from La Opinion Nacional.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >United States of Venezuela—Ministry of the Interior.<lb/>Political
                                Department</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Caracas</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1892-09-17"
                                ><hi rendition="#italic">September 17, 1892</hi></date>. (29 and
                        34.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#italic">Resolved</hi>, It has become known to this ministry that
                    on the 17th of August last there were taken from the American mail steamer <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Caracas</hi>, anchored at Puerto Cabello, Messrs.
                    Jacinto Lopez, Dr. P. Febres Cordero, Francisco M. Casas, Antonio Salinas, M.
                    Lopez, and Manuel Ramos, each of whom had passports duly signed for Curaçao, and
                    as the arrest was executed without orders from the National Executive, officials
                    in all parts of the Republic are hereby directed to give to the above-mentioned
                    citizens, wherever they may find them, every necessary facility to continue on
                    their journey uninterrupted.</p>
                <p>Let it be known and published.</p>
                <p>For the National Executive.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">J. M. Garcia
                    Gomez.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
